ratings, in other words, if it's a valid rating,
then down here, we can basically just say,

04:25:24.550 --> 04:25:33.439
this rating is equal to rating. And the reason
I'm doing this is because if it passes this

04:25:33.439 --> 04:25:37.819
if statement condition, that means it's one
of the valid ratings, so we can just go ahead

04:25:37.819 --> 04:25:42.439
and set it right, that means that they passed
in a valid rating. And we're good to go. Otherwise,

04:25:42.439 --> 04:25:47.049
though, I'm going to create an else block
over here. If they didn't enter in a valid

04:25:47.049 --> 04:25:54.140
rating, why don't we just say this rating
is equal to nr. So why don't we just pass

04:25:54.140 --> 04:25:58.720
it an R. So let's say if they tried to enter
in a rating of like dog or some other nonsense,

04:25:58.720 --> 04:26:02.310
then we're basically just going to say the
movies not rated because they didn't give

04:26:02.310 --> 04:26:07.489
us a valid rating. All right. So the whole
point is that now if I want to set the rating

04:26:07.489 --> 04:26:13.180
to something like dog, it has to go through
this set rating function. And when it goes

04:26:13.180 --> 04:26:17.300
through that set rating function, we have
this if statement, which is going to filter

04:26:17.300 --> 04:26:23.630
out all of the invalid ratings, and set them
equal to n r. So now essentially, what's happening

04:26:23.630 --> 04:26:28.960
is when I run this program, even though I
passed in dog over here, now, when I print

04:26:28.960 --> 04:26:35.710
out the rating, it's going to be an AR. And
that's because I passed in an invalid rating.

04:26:35.710 --> 04:26:40.369
It didn't make it through that if statement,
and it just got set to an R. But if I set

04:26:40.369 --> 04:26:45.080
a valid rating, like if I set this equal to
R, now it's going to be fine with that. So

04:26:45.080 --> 04:26:49.049
it's basically just going to be able to print
out R, because hey, that's a valid rating,

04:26:49.049 --> 04:26:53.629
right? Alright, so that's basically how that
setter is going to work. And there's actually

04:26:53.629 --> 04:26:59.640
one more thing that we have to do. So there's
one problem with this program the way it is,

04:26:59.640 --> 04:27:03.699
and it's this constructor. So the way that
we have the program written, I can come over

04:27:03.699 --> 04:27:08.960
here, and I can still set this equal to dog
by passing it into the constructor, and you'll

04:27:08.960 --> 04:27:14.510
see over here, it's still able to be set to
dog. So the last thing that we can do is just

04:27:14.510 --> 04:27:19.159
come up here to this constructor, and instead
of saying this rating is equal to rating,

04:27:19.159 --> 04:27:29.560
we can just say, this set rating, and we can
pass in, rating into here. So now every place

04:27:29.560 --> 04:27:35.199
where this rating gets set, is going through
this set rating. So now, we're not going to

04:27:35.199 --> 04:27:40.189
be able to get away with putting dog inside
of the constructor. Now it's going to be set

04:27:40.189 --> 04:27:48.369
to not rated like that. So this attribute
is officially locked down, we officially cannot

04:27:48.369 --> 04:27:54.149
give a movie and invalid rating, it's impossible
for me to do that. I cannot give a movie a

04:27:54.149 --> 04:28:00.260
rating other than nr PG, 13, PG or G even
if I try to, like even if I come over here

04:28:00.260 --> 04:28:06.319
in passing into the constructor or I come
down here. And you know, I say like, Avengers

04:28:06.319 --> 04:28:07.319
set rating, and I,

04:28:07.319 --> 04:28:11.850
you know, pass in some nonsense string, that's
not going to work, right. And the way that

04:28:11.850 --> 04:28:18.839
I was able to do that was I made this attribute
private. So I set rating equal to private.

04:28:18.839 --> 04:28:23.720
And I created a getter function, and I created
a setter function, the getter function basically

04:28:23.720 --> 04:28:28.590
just returns the rating. And the setter function
has this if statement, which is going to filter

04:28:28.590 --> 04:28:34.129
out any bad ratings for us, and just set them
equal to nr and then finally, up here in the

04:28:34.129 --> 04:28:40.720
constructor, I called that set rating function.
So that is basically how we can use getters

04:28:40.720 --> 04:28:46.109
and setters. And one important takeaway from
this tutorial was public and private, again,

04:28:46.109 --> 04:28:51.090
before in the tutorial is just using this
var keyword. And again, I was just using that

04:28:51.090 --> 04:28:55.050
because I wanted to explain public and private
In this tutorial, and I don't want you guys

04:28:55.050 --> 04:28:59.879
to get confused. But for the most part, people
are only going to be using public and private

04:28:59.879 --> 04:29:04.819
you won't see people using their too much
although you can use it um, but remember,

04:29:04.819 --> 04:29:10.050
public means that the attribute is open to
anybody any program anywhere. Private though

04:29:10.050 --> 04:29:20.029
means that only code inside the movie class
is going to be able to access this attribute.

04:29:20.029 --> 04:29:24.770
In this tutorial, I'm going to talk to you
guys about inheritance in PHP. Now inheritance

04:29:24.770 --> 04:29:32.350
is basically where a class can inherit all
of the functionality, all the attributes from

04:29:32.350 --> 04:29:38.609
another class in PHP. And this is actually
really useful situation. And the best way

04:29:38.609 --> 04:29:42.220
to kind of wrap your head around inheritance
is just to see an example. So I'm going to

04:29:42.220 --> 04:29:46.829
show you guys an example of what this is how
it works, and then hopefully you can understand

04:29:46.829 --> 04:29:52.859
like basically what's going on. Alright, so
down here in my program, I've created a class

04:29:52.859 --> 04:29:58.449
called chef. And this is basically just like
modeling a chef in my program. And this is

04:29:58.449 --> 04:30:04.129
a very simple class. The chef can do three
things, the chef can make chicken. So we have

04:30:04.129 --> 04:30:09.439
this make chicken function, the chef can make
salad, we have this make salad function. And

04:30:09.439 --> 04:30:14.029
the chef can also make a special dish. And
basically all these functions do is like when

04:30:14.029 --> 04:30:18.239
the chef makes chicken and just prints out,
hey, the chef's making chicken, or when the

04:30:18.239 --> 04:30:22.039
chef makes a salad, it prints out the chef
make salad. And for the special dish, it's

04:30:22.039 --> 04:30:27.290
barbecue ribs. So down here, outside of the
class, I created a chef and I told the chef

04:30:27.290 --> 04:30:31.779
to make some chicken. So now over here, my
web browser, you see when I refresh the page,

04:30:31.779 --> 04:30:37.890
it just says the chef makes chicken. So this
is a you know, like I said, pretty basic program,

04:30:37.890 --> 04:30:42.689
we have our class, it has a bunch of functions
in it, we created an object, and we told the

04:30:42.689 --> 04:30:48.089
object to make chicken. But let's say that
I'm looking at my program. And I'm thinking

04:30:48.089 --> 04:30:56.020
to myself, I want another chef in my program.
So in addition to having this chef class over

04:30:56.020 --> 04:31:01.560
here, let's say that I wanted to create another
chef alongside of it. So let's say in addition

04:31:01.560 --> 04:31:07.310
to just having like a generic chef up here,
I also wanted to have an Italian chef. So

04:31:07.310 --> 04:31:12.819
like a more specialized type of chef, we can
just create another class. So I can just come

04:31:12.819 --> 04:31:18.119
over here and say class, and why don't we
call this Italian chef. And again, I'm going

04:31:18.119 --> 04:31:23.029
to do the same thing, I'm gonna make an open
and closed curly bracket. And down here in

04:31:23.029 --> 04:31:26.680
the Italian chef class, I could you know,
write some functions or some attributes or

04:31:26.680 --> 04:31:32.249
whatever. But let's say that in our program,
we wanted the Italian chef to be able to do

04:31:32.249 --> 04:31:37.100
everything that the normal chef could do plus
a bunch of other stuff, right. So just like

04:31:37.100 --> 04:31:44.609
this normal chef, the Italian chef can also
make chicken make salad and make a special

04:31:44.609 --> 04:31:49.159
dish. So the Italian chef can do everything
that the normal chef can do. And let's say

04:31:49.159 --> 04:31:54.029
the Italian chef can also do some other stuff
as well. Well, this is a situation where we

04:31:54.029 --> 04:31:59.229
can use something called inheritance. And
inheritance is basically where I could have

04:31:59.229 --> 04:32:05.220
my Italian chef, inherit all of the functionality
from the normal chef. So I could just come

04:32:05.220 --> 04:32:12.499
over here and say Italian chef extends chef.
And basically what this means now is the Italian

04:32:12.499 --> 04:32:18.229
chef is going to be able to use all the same
functions as the normal chef, I can actually

04:32:18.229 --> 04:32:22.720
come down here. And in addition to just creating
a normal chef, I'm also going to create an

04:32:22.720 --> 04:32:28.049
Italian chef. So I'm just going to paste this.
And I'm going to change this to Italian chef.

04:32:28.049 --> 04:32:33.779
Alright, so in addition to this normal chef
up here, I also created an Italian chef, it's

04:32:33.779 --> 04:32:39.539
a new Italian chef, and I'm telling this Italian
chef to make chicken. Now here's the thing

04:32:39.539 --> 04:32:44.720
up here in my Italian chef class, I didn't
actually define a make chicken method, like

04:32:44.720 --> 04:32:50.609
nowhere in here does it say, make chicken,
right? It's just not in here. But what I did

04:32:50.609 --> 04:32:57.020
was I extended all the functionality from
the chef class. And the chef class over here

04:32:57.020 --> 04:33:02.989
has a make chicken function. So because the
chef has a make chicken function, and I'm

04:33:02.989 --> 04:33:08.760
inheriting all the functionality from the
chef class, my Italian chef is going to be

04:33:08.760 --> 04:33:14.539
able to make chicken no problem. So now when
I run my program, you'll see it says the chef

04:33:14.539 --> 04:33:19.680
makes chicken and this is that normal chef.
And down here, the Italian chef is also making

04:33:19.680 --> 04:33:24.369
chicken. So without having to write out the
make chicken function down here, I was still

04:33:24.369 --> 04:33:29.469
able to use it because of inheritance. And
that's why inheritance is so cool. But here's

04:33:29.469 --> 04:33:34.420
the thing like this Italian chef, if it only
has the functionality of the chef class, there's

04:33:34.420 --> 04:33:38.238
no purpose for it. So over here, we could
actually create another function, I'm going

04:33:38.238 --> 04:33:43.619
to call this make pasta. So let's say in addition
to making chicken salad and special dish,

04:33:43.619 --> 04:33:48.719
the Italian chef can also make a mean bowl
of pasta. So down here, we could just do basically

04:33:48.719 --> 04:33:55.549
what we did before. So I'm just going to echo
out like so. Now down here, the Italian chef

04:33:55.549 --> 04:34:00.950
could make pasta. But here's the thing, this
normal chef isn't going to be able to make

04:34:00.950 --> 04:34:05.889
pasta. So I couldn't come over here and say
normal chef make pasta, this is actually going

04:34:05.889 --> 04:34:12.560
to throw an error. And that's because the
chef doesn't have a make pasta method. Only

04:34:12.560 --> 04:34:19.020
the Italian chef is capable of making pasta,
because only the Italian chef has that function.

04:34:19.020 --> 04:34:23.750
So over here now when I refresh the page,
you can see the Italian chef is making pasta.

04:34:23.750 --> 04:34:29.160
Anytime we have two classes like the chef
and the Italian chef, where the Italian chef

04:34:29.160 --> 04:34:34.490
can do everything the chef can do plus some
other stuff. We want to use inheritance and

04:34:34.490 --> 04:34:39.539
this can be extremely powerful. I want to
show you guys one more thing that we can do

04:34:39.539 --> 04:34:43.539
with this though and it's called overriding
a function. So down here I'm going to have

04:34:43.539 --> 04:34:49.979
both of these guys print out make special
dish so both chefs are going to go ahead and

04:34:49.979 --> 04:34:56.709
make a special dish and you'll see that both
of them are going to make barbecue ribs. So

04:34:56.709 --> 04:35:02.900
the special dish is barbecue ribs and then
the Italian Chef also makes barbecue ribs.

04:35:02.900 --> 04:35:07.160
But let's say that the Italian chef doesn't
want to make barbecue ribs. So the normal

04:35:07.160 --> 04:35:11.580
chef's special dishes, barbecue ribs, that's
awesome. But let's say that we want the Italian

04:35:11.580 --> 04:35:17.449
chef to have a different special dish. Well,
I can actually do something called overriding

04:35:17.449 --> 04:35:23.939
a function. Basically down here, I could override
the make special dysfunction that I inherited

04:35:23.939 --> 04:35:27.459
from the chef class. And it's actually really
easy. All I have to do is just come down here

04:35:27.459 --> 04:35:34.299
and I could just say function make special
dish. So you'll notice I'm using the same

04:35:34.299 --> 04:35:40.279
name as the function that was up here in the
chef class. And then over here, I can basically

04:35:40.279 --> 04:35:47.719
just put my own thing, so I could just say
like Echo, the chef makes chicken parm, maybe

04:35:47.719 --> 04:35:54.010
that's the chef's special dish. So now when
I come down here, and I say, Chef make special

04:35:54.010 --> 04:35:58.439
dish and Italian chef make special dish, they're
both going to be making different special

04:35:58.439 --> 04:36:04.350
dishes. So now the Italian chef makes chicken
parm. And the normal chef makes barbecue ribs.

04:36:04.350 --> 04:36:09.410
And that's what we would call overriding a
function. Basically, we're overriding the

04:36:09.410 --> 04:36:16.000
make special dish function that we got from
the chef when we use inheritance. And that

04:36:16.000 --> 04:36:19.811
can come in handy all the time. So that's
sort of the basics of inheritance. And really,

04:36:19.811 --> 04:36:25.229
the basics of using this extends keyword as
your PHP programs get more complex. And as

04:36:25.229 --> 04:36:31.559
you start using more and more complex classes,
using something like inheritance can become

04:36:31.559 --> 04:36:36.920
very useful. So hopefully all that makes sense
